  - path: "docs/architecture.html"
    tier: T1
    action: updated
    distills: "The epic's one T1 edit, rule-bearing and committed by the owner through `cargo xtask mr commit --as vuluu` - staged and printed by the agent, never run by it. Three sentences. The servers paragraph no longer says the runtime hosts both servers 'on loopback' as a flat fact: they bind loopback BY DEFAULT and a user may add a second pair on one named interface, an authorization act costing one explicit confirm, off by default and dead with the process (DEC-55) - with the reason the loopback pair is never displaced, which is that the app's own client cannot be allowed to lose its connection to a disarm. The CORS guardrail row is restated against the new boundary: it was justified by 'adding no exposure beyond the bind address', DEC-55 retires that sentence because the bind is now the user's to widen, and what bounds exposure instead is the confirm - the grant does NOT widen with the bind. The row also now states what arming exposes (the proxy and the whole control API, captured flows included) rather than implying it, and says that anything finer is deliberately absent and its own epic. The run-the-core row gains one clause: reach is off until something arms it."
  - path: "docs/backlog/roadmap.html"
    tier: T2
    action: updated
    distills: "Six rows moved and three added. STRUCK: the E-ONBOARD row, with what the six WUs actually did and - the part a strike usually loses - what did NOT travel with it, so the residue is visible rather than implied. FILLED: the flow's clock-and-token bill row, E-SIMPDF's expected result 6, with W2's measured round and its three readings, and with W4's round recorded as unmeasured rather than estimated. UPDATED WITH EVIDENCE RATHER THAN CLOSURE: overflow-declared's elective gap - the first UI epic to run the new flow did not meet it, because the run declared per zone and argued the cap (five zones bounded by content, steps named as the one unbounded zone, its cap reasoned to the page because neither StepList nor Disclosure may cap its own content), so the rule got the answer it wants from an author who engaged with it and the gap is that nothing would have asked had they not. WIDENED: the WebdriverIO row, which was about one silent harness failure and is now about two - the *= selector, which W6 followed the remedy for and which did not bite, and the pnpm pin that stopped applying, which is why the suite had not booted at all. ADDED: the quit dialog as its own successor row, naming wu/redesign-finish/onboarding-design @ 7c6e144 as its only carrier twice and the three items travelling with it; the six untaken P2 findings as one row with each named, what it asks for, and for F19 the rule any fix must not break; the iOS Simulator's proxy-reversal latch with why W5 refused it twice and what a fix owes at the seam; and the attribution row - no traffic reading says which device a connection came from - with the two shipped sentences that are weaker because of it. RE-POINTED: the closing next-epic sentence, which said E-ONBOARD was unblocked and holding a parked run."
  - path: "docs/decisions/dec-56-the-app-may-prove-capture-itself.html"
    tier: T2
    action: added
    distills: "The PO review's F24 as a capability decision rather than a button. The app MAY fire one HTTPS request through the system proxy to prove setup, on an explicit click and never on its own - it proves the certificate and the proxy at once, because a request it makes can only be decrypted if both are in place. Five rules a build owes: the button produces evidence and never a state, so the task's reading stays the traffic layer's and DEC-33's no-self-reported-tick rule survives; the destination is visible in the sentence and not owned by Trawler, because a request to an address the app chose in secret is a fact the user cannot check; it never runs automatically, on mount or as part of the set-up action, which is what keeps it a convenience rather than a privacy question; a failure says what failed and leaves the manual routes standing; and the two manual routes stay, because the button is a third. No confirm, with the reason: unlike the trust store and the system proxy, one outbound request changes nothing about this host. Three things it does not decide, each named with who decides it - and one it does decide by implication and states rather than leaving to be found: the button is the host platforms', because a request this app makes travels through this host and cannot prove a phone's setup. Marked proposed, not built."
  - path: "docs/decisions/dec-57-a-device-that-cannot-paste.html"
    tier: T2
    action: added
    distills: "The PO review's F28 as a capability decision. DEC-55 gave the five off-machine platforms something to point at; what it could not give them is a way to get it there, because the clipboard does not cross to a phone - so a host, a port and a certificate URL are typed by hand, and one wrong character in a URL fails exactly like an untrusted certificate. A value that must cross to a device this machine cannot reach may be rendered as a scannable code BESIDE the value, never instead of it. Five rules: the typed route stays, because a code is unreadable to a human and a screen showing only the code has made a fact uncheckable; a code is rendered only where reach is armed and only for a value that must travel; it is generated locally from a vendored dependency and never fetched from a hosted renderer, because that would hand the user's own network address to a third party and make an offline app need the internet to finish setup; it is a registry component with its own contract per DEC-16, since a new visual primitive appearing inside one screen's build with no reusable contract is what the design flow exists to prevent; and it encodes exactly the value beside it. Marked proposed, not built."
  - path: "docs/decisions/decisions-index.html"
    tier: T2
    action: updated
    distills: "Two rows, for DEC-56 and DEC-57, each carrying the decision, its rules, its rejected alternatives and the PO finding it came from - and each badged `proposed` rather than `active`, because neither is built and an index that cannot tell those apart is an index that lies about what the code does. DEC-55's row was VERIFIED rather than written: the epic plan assigns it to W6, it landed complete with the record at W1, it was read against what DEC-55 decides, and nothing was changed. A row written twice is worse than a row written once, and the result records it as met at W1 rather than silently leaving the plan's item unaccounted."
  - path: "docs/design-rules/design-rules-index.html"
    tier: T2
    action: updated
    distills: "The Lexicon gains the design run's two proposals and one scoping clause, which is the whole of what this epic owed the rulebook. `arriving` - a connection Trawler received and could not decrypt - exists because the traffic layer can observe a tunnel before any flow does, so the screen needed a word for traffic that is real, is here, and has produced nothing to read; its Never column names connected, incoming, pending, blocked and failed, none of which is true of a tunnel that opened. `platform` - the one thing a run of setup is for - is the setup screen's only axis and deliberately spans kinds the app treats alike, so its Never column is device, target, OS and environment, each of which names one kind and excludes the others. And the existing `flow` entry gains the scoping W5 made true in code: it is the capture's word and the setup screen may not use it, because a user who has captured nothing has no referent for it - so `flow` now appears in its own Never column, scoped to that screen. Rounds 5 and 6 proposed no vocabulary, which the W5 result recorded as a statement rather than an omission, so this track looked for nothing beyond these three rows."
  - path: "docs/use-cases/use-cases-index.html"
    tier: T2
    action: updated
    distills: "UC-ONBOARD's one-line scope, which described guided onboarding with two-truths per-step status, now describes what the screen is: setting up capture for this machine or another device, the platform as the only axis, reach as an opt-in one confirm buys and the process ends, a task's state from a probe or the traffic layer or the reach this app holds and never from a click, and only a decrypted flow revealing the list. Version bumped."
  - path: "docs/use-cases/web-ui.html"
    tier: T2
    action: updated
    distills: "UC-ONBOARD RESTATED ONCE, against the tip - the deferral W4 and W5 each made for the same reason (a contract restated against a screen the epic is still closing around is a contract written twice) is discharged here, and W5's distillation section is the source it reads. The old section described a three-step guided setup - Trust the CA, Set the proxy, Make a request - which the screen has not been since W3. What replaces it: the platform axis as the screen's only axis; a table of eight platforms against what each may promise, because what the screen may say is decided by what it can observe and not by what would be convenient; reach as its own block (DEC-55, the loopback pair never touched, dimmed dependants rather than steps that cannot work, and the CORS grant not widening); the two layers restated in the screen's own terms with the probes and the traffic reading kept strictly apart; four acts tabled with what each costs and what it says afterwards, including the two confirms W5 built and the reversal that names the half it performs; and a table of how much is on screen at once - one guide open, a command on its own surface, a caveat under the steps, the column answering to the window. THE NO-NARROWING STATEMENT IS EXPLICIT AND NEAR THE TOP, because that is the failure mode a shortening restatement has: DEC-33 and DEC-13 are both kept and both re-asserted by test, the probes still answer for this machine only, a decrypted flow is still the only thing that reveals the list, the save still reports what actually happened, and the quit dialog is untouched. A `what the rebuild kept` table carries those five guarantees with the mechanism for each, so nothing was dropped by being left out. The flow diagram is redrawn from the platform question down. The provenance callout adds GET/POST/DELETE /reach to the API surface, states that the same DEC-13 boundary keeping host-reading out of the core is why the shell raises every confirm, and re-points Pinned by at the rewritten spine BY JOURNEY NAME - including which half of journey 2 is driven at integration and why. The doc's intro line and meta version move with it."
  - path: "docs/work/2026-09-21-onboard-close-plan.html"
    tier: T3
    action: added
    distills: "W6's plan. Five tracks, ordered because the e2e is the only one that can discover something and a red spine changes what every later document claims. It carries the coverage map as a plan rather than as a result - each of the 8 assertions with its intended disposition - so the rewrite could be judged against a commitment rather than against itself. Its one callout is the journey-2 deviation, found while planning rather than while building: the three facts that make arming undrivable, each with the file it lives in, and the refusal of the e2e-feature stub with its reason. It also predicts the one production edit the track may make (a test hook on an element that already renders) and says what the result must state if none is needed."
  - path: "docs/work/2026-09-21-onboard-epic-result.html"
    tier: T3
    action: added
    distills: "The epic result. Six WUs rolled up with what each landed and what its review cost - 11 review rounds, five cr/v5 PASS notes each bound by diff_sha256 to the range it judged, 0 findings verified at any WU's final round. THE BILL, which is the epic's standing deliverable: W2's round measured end to end (78 min 41 s elapsed, agent working 18 min 40 s over 119 turns for 145,162 output tokens against 19.2M cache read) with its three readings - the human gate is 76% of elapsed time so a cut that saves agent effort saves almost nothing; output and cache are not one number and a total that adds them reports cache traffic as design effort; and a review round costs about what the draft did, because answering a call well means re-reading a contract. W4's round is BLANK and recorded as blank: no clock was started, this project writes no estimates, and a reconstructed figure would have been indistinguishable from the measured row beside it - which is also the process finding, that a round not instrumented when it runs cannot be billed afterwards. The result adds one reading the flow's own bill cannot: four more design readings happened outside the flow (the PO review, W4's end-user reading, the owner's two at the window), and that is where most of the epic's design change came from, so a bill covering only what runs before a build measures the smaller half. Seven deviations, including this WU's journey-2 half, the registry moving where the plan predicted stillness (two entries built, one withdrawn - the plan's conclusion holds and its reason does not), and DEC-55's index row verified rather than written."
